{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01GQ4Z8U2PyRWoyeYUQKnKuf","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-XhyS4a/translated_rust/SYMBOLS.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f9439a60-726c-49d3-8b44-096a552fc68e","uuid":"51fedea9-c2aa-4271-a459-8a61b548d832","timestamp":"2026-08-18T03:58:55.294Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-XhyS4a/translated_rust/SYMBOLS.md","oldString":"**Result: 0 symbols missing from the Rust `.so`.** (Reproduced by\n`tests/symbol_parity.rs::c_exported_symbols_are_all_exported_by_rust`, which\nshells out to `nm -D` and asserts the diff is empty, and by\n`tests/differential.rs`, which resolves all 5 symbols in both libraries via\n`libloading` — a symbol that were missing would fail to resolve.)","newString":"**Result: 0 symbols missing from the Rust `.so`.** (Reproduced by\n`tests/phase_d_symbols.rs::c_exported_symbols_are_all_exported_by_rust`, which\nshells out to `nm -D` and asserts the diff is empty, and by\n`harness::Api::load`, which resolves all 5 symbols in both libraries via\n`libloading` during every test — a missing symbol would fail to resolve.)","originalFile":"# SYMBOLS.md — Phase A symbol surface\n\nDerived mechanically from `nm -D` on both shared objects.\n\n## Build commands\n\n```\n# C\ncd c_src && mkdir -p build && cd build \\\n  && cmake .. -DCMAKE_POSITION_INDEPENDENT_CODE=ON && cmake --build .\n# -> c_src/build/libdriver.so\n\n# Rust (only one configuration exists; see CONFIGS.md \"Feature combinations\")\ncargo build --no-default-features            # -> target/debug/libdriver.so\n```\n\n## C source inventory (completeness check)\n\n`c_src/CMakeLists.txt` compiles exactly one translation unit:\n\n| C file | translated to | status |\n|--------|---------------|--------|\n| `c_src/src/driver.c` (99 lines) | `src/lib.rs` | fully translated |\n| `c_src/include/driver.h` (29 lines) | declarations mirrored in `src/lib.rs` | fully translated |\n\nNo C source file is missing from the Rust translation, so no Phase A\n\"translate the missing module\" work is required.\n\n## Exported (defined) dynamic symbols\n\n`nm -D --defined-only <so>`, function/data symbols only:\n\n| # | symbol | C `.so` | Rust `.so` | C declaration | notes |\n|---|--------|---------|------------|---------------|-------|\n| 1 | `printLine`        | T | T | `void printLine(const char *line)` | `driver.c:30` |\n| 2 | `printHexCharLine` | T | T | `void printHexCharLine(char charHex)` | `driver.c:38` |\n| 3 | `bad`              | T | T | `void bad(void)`  | `driver.c:43` |\n| 4 | `good`             | T | T | `void good(void)` | `driver.c:84` |\n| 5 | `driver`           | T | T | `void driver(int useGood)` | `driver.c:90`, the only symbol in `driver.h` |\n\n### Deliberately NOT exported (`static` in C, private in Rust)\n\n| C symbol | linkage | Rust counterpart |\n|----------|---------|------------------|\n| `goodG2B` | `static void goodG2B()` — internal | `unsafe fn goodG2B()` (private, not `#[no_mangle]`) |\n| `goodB2G` | `static void goodB2G()` — internal | `unsafe fn goodB2G()` (private, not `#[no_mangle]`) |\n\nThese are correctly absent from both `.so` files. Adding `#[no_mangle]`\nwrappers for them would be a *divergence*, not a fix.\n\n## Symbol diff\n\n```\n$ diff <(nm -D --defined-only c_src/build/libdriver.so | awk '{print $NF}' | sort) \\\n       <(nm -D --defined-only target/debug/libdriver.so \\\n           | grep -E ' (T|B|D|W|R) ' | awk '{print $NF}' | sort)\n(empty)\n```\n\n**Result: 0 symbols missing from the Rust `.so`.** (Reproduced by\n`tests/symbol_parity.rs::c_exported_symbols_are_all_exported_by_rust`, which\nshells out to `nm -D` and asserts the diff is empty, and by\n`tests/differential.rs`, which resolves all 5 symbols in both libraries via\n`libloading` — a symbol that were missing would fail to resolve.)\n\n## Undefined symbols in the Rust `.so`\n\n`nm -D -u target/debug/libdriver.so` lists only libc / libgcc-unwind imports:\n\n`_ITM_deregisterTMCloneTable`, `_ITM_registerTMCloneTable`, `_Unwind_*@GCC_*`,\n`__cxa_finalize`, `__cxa_thread_atexit_impl`, `__errno_location`,\n`__gmon_start__`, `__tls_get_addr`, `abort`, `bcmp`, `calloc`, `close`,\n`dl_iterate_phdr`, `free`, `fstat64`, `getcwd`, `getenv`, `gettid`, `lseek64`,\n`malloc`, `memcpy`, `memmove`, `memset`, `mmap64`, `munmap`, `open64`,\n`posix_memalign`, **`printf@GLIBC_2.2.5`**, `pthread_key_create`,\n`pthread_key_delete`, `pthread_setspecific`, `read`, `readlink`, `realloc`,\n`realpath`, `stat64`, `statx`, `strlen`, `syscall`, `write`, `writev`.\n\n**0 missing/undefined non-libc symbols.** The only *intentional* import that\nmatters for behaviour is `printf@GLIBC_2.2.5`: the Rust translation calls the\nsame variadic libc `printf` as the C code, through the same process-global\n`stdout` `FILE`, so formatting, integer promotion and stdio buffering are\nbyte-identical by construction rather than re-implemented.\n\n## ABI check on the narrow (`char`) parameter\n\n`printHexCharLine` takes a `char`. Under the SysV AMD64 ABI the caller may\nleave the upper 24 bits of `%edi` undefined, so the callee must narrow. Both\nimplementations do, identically:\n\n| | narrowing instruction |\n|---|---|\n| C (`gcc -O0`) | `mov %edi,%eax; mov %al,-0x4(%rbp); movsbl -0x4(%rbp),%eax` |\n| Rust (debug)  | `mov %dil,%al; mov %al,0x6(%rsp); mov 0x6(%rsp),%al; movsbl %al,%esi` |\n\nBoth keep only the low byte and sign-extend it. This is asserted behaviourally\nby the `dirty_upper_bits_*` tests, which call the symbol through a deliberately\nwidened `extern \"C\" fn(c_int)` prototype.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":58,"oldLines":10,"newStart":58,"newLines":10,"lines":[" ```"," "," **Result: 0 symbols missing from the Rust `.so`.** (Reproduced by","-`tests/symbol_parity.rs::c_exported_symbols_are_all_exported_by_rust`, which","+`tests/phase_d_symbols.rs::c_exported_symbols_are_all_exported_by_rust`, which"," shells out to `nm -D` and asserts the diff is empty, and by","-`tests/differential.rs`, which resolves all 5 symbols in both libraries via","-`libloading` — a symbol that were missing would fail to resolve.)","+`harness::Api::load`, which resolves all 5 symbols in both libraries via","+`libloading` during every test — a missing symbol would fail to resolve.)"," "," ## Undefined symbols in the Rust `.so`"," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_3wmkohqwyw425k7s6tacy7vjs37hpdakkwvcdeys7pcdpfpkzldq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01HyVkjmFXPwAvHxPanfLHC6","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-XhyS4a/translated_rust/SYMBOLS.md","old_string":"## ABI check on the narrow (`char`) parameter\n\n`printHexCharLine` takes a `char`. Under the SysV AMD64 ABI the caller may\nleave the upper 24 bits of `%edi` undefined, so the callee must narrow. Both\nimplementations do, identically:\n\n| | narrowing instruction |\n|---|---|\n| C (`gcc -O0`) | `mov %edi,%eax; mov %al,-0x4(%rbp); movsbl -0x4(%rbp),%eax` |\n| Rust (debug)  | `mov %dil,%al; mov %al,0x6(%rsp); mov 0x6(%rsp),%al; movsbl %al,%esi` |\n\nBoth keep only the low byte and sign-extend it. This is asserted behaviourally\nby the `dirty_upper_bits_*` tests, which call the symbol through a deliberately\nwidened `extern \"C\" fn(c_int)` prototype.","new_string":"## ABI check on the narrow (`char`) parameter\n\n`printHexCharLine` takes a `char`. Under the SysV AMD64 psABI the caller may\nleave the upper 24 bits of `%edi` undefined, so the callee must narrow. gcc does\nso at every optimisation level; the Rust originally did **not** once optimised.\nThat was a real divergence and it was fixed — see the \"Divergence found and\nfixed (row E9)\" section of `ERRORS.md`. Final state:\n\n| build | narrowing instruction | narrows? |\n|-------|----------------------|----------|\n| C, `cmake` default (`-O0`) | `mov %edi,%eax; mov %al,-0x4(%rbp); movsbl -0x4(%rbp),%eax` | yes |\n| C, `gcc -O2` | `movsbl %dil,%esi` | yes |\n| Rust `dev` (after fix) | `movsbl` on the low byte | yes |\n| Rust `release` (after fix) | `movsbl %dil,%esi` | yes — instruction-identical to `gcc -O2` |\n\nAsserted behaviourally by\n`tests/phase_c_errors.rs::e9_print_hex_char_line_out_of_range_int_across_ffi`\nand `generic_one_step_past_every_documented_range`, which call the symbol\nthrough a deliberately widened `extern \"C\" fn(c_int)` prototype, and by\n`tests/phase_d_symbols.rs::optimised_c_build_agrees_with_rust_on_the_whole_surface`,\nwhich compares against a `-O2` rebuild of the C.\n\n## Codegen parity note: `printf` → `puts`\n\nOptimised builds rewrite `printf(\"%s\\n\", s)` into `puts(s)`. `gcc -O2` performs\nthe *identical* transform on `printLine` (`test %rdi,%rdi; je; jmp puts@plt`),\nso the `puts` import that appears in the release Rust `.so`'s undefined-symbol\nlist is codegen parity with the optimised C, not a behavioural change. Confirmed\nbyte-identical for NULL, empty, `%`-bearing, non-UTF-8 and 70 KB payloads."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1161,"cache_read_input_tokens":148884,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1161,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f9439a60-726c-49d3-8b44-096a552fc68e","uuid":"3149abf1-8b7f-4686-83a5-bdcc5f06122f","timestamp":"2026-08-18T03:59:09.074Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01HyVkjmFXPwAvHxPanfLHC6","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-XhyS4a/translated_rust/SYMBOLS.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f9439a60-726c-49d3-8b44-096a552fc68e","uuid":"f95e6f78-76fd-45ff-b3fc-672baaf27d21","timestamp":"2026-08-18T03:59:09.084Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-XhyS4a/translated_rust/SYMBOLS.md","oldString":"## ABI check on the narrow (`char`) parameter\n\n`printHexCharLine` takes a `char`. Under the SysV AMD64 ABI the caller may\nleave the upper 24 bits of `%edi` undefined, so the callee must narrow. Both\nimplementations do, identically:\n\n| | narrowing instruction |\n|---|---|\n| C (`gcc -O0`) | `mov %edi,%eax; mov %al,-0x4(%rbp); movsbl -0x4(%rbp),%eax` |\n| Rust (debug)  | `mov %dil,%al; mov %al,0x6(%rsp); mov 0x6(%rsp),%al; movsbl %al,%esi` |\n\nBoth keep only the low byte and sign-extend it. This is asserted behaviourally\nby the `dirty_upper_bits_*` tests, which call the symbol through a deliberately\nwidened `extern \"C\" fn(c_int)` prototype.","newString":"## ABI check on the narrow (`char`) parameter\n\n`printHexCharLine` takes a `char`. Under the SysV AMD64 psABI the caller may\nleave the upper 24 bits of `%edi` undefined, so the callee must narrow. gcc does\nso at every optimisation level; the Rust originally did **not** once optimised.\nThat was a real divergence and it was fixed — see the \"Divergence found and\nfixed (row E9)\" section of `ERRORS.md`. Final state:\n\n| build | narrowing instruction | narrows? |\n|-------|----------------------|----------|\n| C, `cmake` default (`-O0`) | `mov %edi,%eax; mov %al,-0x4(%rbp); movsbl -0x4(%rbp),%eax` | yes |\n| C, `gcc -O2` | `movsbl %dil,%esi` | yes |\n| Rust `dev` (after fix) | `movsbl` on the low byte | yes |\n| Rust `release` (after fix) | `movsbl %dil,%esi` | yes — instruction-identical to `gcc -O2` |\n\nAsserted behaviourally by\n`tests/phase_c_errors.rs::e9_print_hex_char_line_out_of_range_int_across_ffi`\nand `generic_one_step_past_every_documented_range`, which call the symbol\nthrough a deliberately widened `extern \"C\" fn(c_int)` prototype, and by\n`tests/phase_d_symbols.rs::optimised_c_build_agrees_with_rust_on_the_whole_surface`,\nwhich compares against a `-O2` rebuild of the C.\n\n## Codegen parity note: `printf` → `puts`\n\nOptimised builds rewrite `printf(\"%s\\n\", s)` into `puts(s)`. `gcc -O2` performs\nthe *identical* transform on `printLine` (`test %rdi,%rdi; je; jmp puts@plt`),\nso the `puts` import that appears in the release Rust `.so`'s undefined-symbol\nlist is codegen parity with the optimised C, not a behavioural change. Confirmed\nbyte-identical for NULL, empty, `%`-bearing, non-UTF-8 and 70 KB payloads.","originalFile":"# SYMBOLS.md — Phase A symbol surface\n\nDerived mechanically from `nm -D` on both shared objects.\n\n## Build commands\n\n```\n# C\ncd c_src && mkdir -p build && cd build \\\n  && cmake .. -DCMAKE_POSITION_INDEPENDENT_CODE=ON && cmake --build .\n# -> c_src/build/libdriver.so\n\n# Rust (only one configuration exists; see CONFIGS.md \"Feature combinations\")\ncargo build --no-default-features            # -> target/debug/libdriver.so\n```\n\n## C source inventory (completeness check)\n\n`c_src/CMakeLists.txt` compiles exactly one translation unit:\n\n| C file | translated to | status |\n|--------|---------------|--------|\n| `c_src/src/driver.c` (99 lines) | `src/lib.rs` | fully translated |\n| `c_src/include/driver.h` (29 lines) | declarations mirrored in `src/lib.rs` | fully translated |\n\nNo C source file is missing from the Rust translation, so no Phase A\n\"translate the missing module\" work is required.\n\n## Exported (defined) dynamic symbols\n\n`nm -D --defined-only <so>`, function/data symbols only:\n\n| # | symbol | C `.so` | Rust `.so` | C declaration | notes |\n|---|--------|---------|------------|---------------|-------|\n| 1 | `printLine`        | T | T | `void printLine(const char *line)` | `driver.c:30` |\n| 2 | `printHexCharLine` | T | T | `void printHexCharLine(char charHex)` | `driver.c:38` |\n| 3 | `bad`              | T | T | `void bad(void)`  | `driver.c:43` |\n| 4 | `good`             | T | T | `void good(void)` | `driver.c:84` |\n| 5 | `driver`           | T | T | `void driver(int useGood)` | `driver.c:90`, the only symbol in `driver.h` |\n\n### Deliberately NOT exported (`static` in C, private in Rust)\n\n| C symbol | linkage | Rust counterpart |\n|----------|---------|------------------|\n| `goodG2B` | `static void goodG2B()` — internal | `unsafe fn goodG2B()` (private, not `#[no_mangle]`) |\n| `goodB2G` | `static void goodB2G()` — internal | `unsafe fn goodB2G()` (private, not `#[no_mangle]`) |\n\nThese are correctly absent from both `.so` files. Adding `#[no_mangle]`\nwrappers for them would be a *divergence*, not a fix.\n\n## Symbol diff\n\n```\n$ diff <(nm -D --defined-only c_src/build/libdriver.so | awk '{print $NF}' | sort) \\\n       <(nm -D --defined-only target/debug/libdriver.so \\\n           | grep -E ' (T|B|D|W|R) ' | awk '{print $NF}' | sort)\n(empty)\n```\n\n**Result: 0 symbols missing from the Rust `.so`.** (Reproduced by\n`tests/phase_d_symbols.rs::c_exported_symbols_are_all_exported_by_rust`, which\nshells out to `nm -D` and asserts the diff is empty, and by\n`harness::Api::load`, which resolves all 5 symbols in both libraries via\n`libloading` during every test — a missing symbol would fail to resolve.)\n\n## Undefined symbols in the Rust `.so`\n\n`nm -D -u target/debug/libdriver.so` lists only libc / libgcc-unwind imports:\n\n`_ITM_deregisterTMCloneTable`, `_ITM_registerTMCloneTable`, `_Unwind_*@GCC_*`,\n`__cxa_finalize`, `__cxa_thread_atexit_impl`, `__errno_location`,\n`__gmon_start__`, `__tls_get_addr`, `abort`, `bcmp`, `calloc`, `close`,\n`dl_iterate_phdr`, `free`, `fstat64`, `getcwd`, `getenv`, `gettid`, `lseek64`,\n`malloc`, `memcpy`, `memmove`, `memset`, `mmap64`, `munmap`, `open64`,\n`posix_memalign`, **`printf@GLIBC_2.2.5`**, `pthread_key_create`,\n`pthread_key_delete`, `pthread_setspecific`, `read`, `readlink`, `realloc`,\n`realpath`, `stat64`, `statx`, `strlen`, `syscall`, `write`, `writev`.\n\n**0 missing/undefined non-libc symbols.** The only *intentional* import that\nmatters for behaviour is `printf@GLIBC_2.2.5`: the Rust translation calls the\nsame variadic libc `printf` as the C code, through the same process-global\n`stdout` `FILE`, so formatting, integer promotion and stdio buffering are\nbyte-identical by construction rather than re-implemented.\n\n## ABI check on the narrow (`char`) parameter\n\n`printHexCharLine` takes a `char`. Under the SysV AMD64 ABI the caller may\nleave the upper 24 bits of `%edi` undefined, so the callee must narrow. Both\nimplementations do, identically:\n\n| | narrowing instruction |\n|---|---|\n| C (`gcc -O0`) | `mov %edi,%eax; mov %al,-0x4(%rbp); movsbl -0x4(%rbp),%eax` |\n| Rust (debug)  | `mov %dil,%al; mov %al,0x6(%rsp); mov 0x6(%rsp),%al; movsbl %al,%esi` |\n\nBoth keep only the low byte and sign-extend it. This is asserted behaviourally\nby the `dirty_upper_bits_*` tests, which call the symbol through a deliberately\nwidened `extern \"C\" fn(c_int)` prototype.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":84,"oldLines":15,"newStart":84,"newLines":30,"lines":[" "," ## ABI check on the narrow (`char`) parameter"," ","-`printHexCharLine` takes a `char`. Under the SysV AMD64 ABI the caller may","-leave the upper 24 bits of `%edi` undefined, so the callee must narrow. Both","-implementations do, identically:","+`printHexCharLine` takes a `char`. Under the SysV AMD64 psABI the caller may","+leave the upper 24 bits of `%edi` undefined, so the callee must narrow. gcc does","+so at every optimisation level; the Rust originally did **not** once optimised.","+That was a real divergence and it was fixed — see the \"Divergence found and","+fixed (row E9)\" section of `ERRORS.md`. Final state:"," ","-| | narrowing instruction |","-|---|---|","-| C (`gcc -O0`) | `mov %edi,%eax; mov %al,-0x4(%rbp); movsbl -0x4(%rbp),%eax` |","-| Rust (debug)  | `mov %dil,%al; mov %al,0x6(%rsp); mov 0x6(%rsp),%al; movsbl %al,%esi` |","+| build | narrowing instruction | narrows? |","+|-------|----------------------|----------|","+| C, `cmake` default (`-O0`) | `mov %edi,%eax; mov %al,-0x4(%rbp); movsbl -0x4(%rbp),%eax` | yes |","+| C, `gcc -O2` | `movsbl %dil,%esi` | yes |","+| Rust `dev` (after fix) | `movsbl` on the low byte | yes |","+| Rust `release` (after fix) | `movsbl %dil,%esi` | yes — instruction-identical to `gcc -O2` |"," ","-Both keep only the low byte and sign-extend it. This is asserted behaviourally","-by the `dirty_upper_bits_*` tests, which call the symbol through a deliberately","-widened `extern \"C\" fn(c_int)` prototype.","+Asserted behaviourally by","+`tests/phase_c_errors.rs::e9_print_hex_char_line_out_of_range_int_across_ffi`","+and `generic_one_step_past_every_documented_range`, which call the symbol","+through a deliberately widened `extern \"C\" fn(c_int)` prototype, and by","+`tests/phase_d_symbols.rs::optimised_c_build_agrees_with_rust_on_the_whole_surface`,","+which compares against a `-O2` rebuild of the C.","+","+## Codegen parity note: `printf` → `puts`","+","+Optimised builds rewrite `printf(\"%s\\n\", s)` into `puts(s)`. `gcc -O2` performs","+the *identical* transform on `printLine` (`test %rdi,%rdi; je; jmp puts@plt`),","+so the `puts` import that appears in the release Rust `.so`'s undefined-symbol","+list is codegen parity with the optimised C, not a behavioural change. Confirmed","+byte-identical for NULL, empty, `%`-bearing, non-UTF-8 and 70 KB payloads."]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
