{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_uvcvitmg6khetixsq7hnakd5n4ltgtfnworsgm6v4afrdhblecuq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Everything passes from a fully clean tree. Verification is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe library (`c_src/src/driver.c`, a CWE-190 signed-char-overflow demo) exports 5 symbols. I verified the Rust translation against it through the `.so` boundary in all four phases.\n\n**Phase A — artifacts** (`SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md`, `CONFIGS.md`, all derived mechanically from the C source):\n- **Feature combinations:** `Cargo.toml` has **no `[features]` section** and `CMakeLists.txt` has no `option()`/`-D` defines, so the complete enumeration is the *single empty combination*. `run_all_configs.sh` re-verifies that enumeration is still exhaustive on every run rather than assuming it. I added the dev/release profile axis as a second dimension, since `[profile.release] panic = \"abort\"` and `debug_assertions` genuinely change behavior here.\n- **Symbol parity was already complete** (5/5), and `goodG2B`/`goodB2G` are correctly absent from *both* — exporting them would have been a divergence, so I assert their absence too.\n- `ERRORS.md`: 20 rejection rows; `CONFIGS.md`: 16 configuration rows.\n\n**Phases B/C/D — 56 tests** (`tests/`), loading **both** `.so`s via `libloading` and comparing captured `stdout` bytes. Since every function returns `void`, \"same result\" means byte-identical fd-1 output, captured with `dup2`/`ftruncate` and flushed through the shared libc `stdout`.\n\n### One real bug found and fixed\n\n`printHexCharLine(char)` — declaring the Rust parameter as `c_char` makes rustc attach LLVM's `signext` attribute, i.e. a promise that the *caller* sign-extended the byte. An optimized build then forwards the full 32-bit register (`mov %edi,%esi`), whereas **gcc narrows defensively at every `-O` level** (`movsbl`). Result: identical in debug, divergent in release.\n\nFixed by taking `c_int` and narrowing explicitly; the optimized Rust now emits `movsbl %dil,%esi` — the instruction-identical sequence to `gcc -O2`.\n\nThis is exactly the failure mode the instructions warn about: symbols matched and 54 happy-path tests passed while the bug was still present. It only surfaced because I ran the *release* profile as a separate configuration. I then added a test that rebuilds the C at `-O2` (writing only into `target/`, never `c_src/`) so the bug class stays covered against both C optimization levels.\n\n### Guarding against a vacuous suite\n\nA suite that passes on the first try is not evidence, so `mutation_check.sh` injects 15 deliberate bugs and requires the suite to fail for each — **all 15 caught in both profiles**, pristine passes afterward. Three of my own mistakes surfaced here and were fixed:\n- a mutation that only edited a doc comment (now rejected as `COMMENT-ONLY`);\n- a mutation that was a semantic no-op (`as u8 as c_int as c_char` ≡ `as u8 as c_char`);\n- a detection gap where the suite *did* catch a bug via `SIGSEGV` in `puts(NULL)` but my grep missed the signal death — now keyed off cargo's exit code.\n\nI also confirmed the `puts` import in the release `.so` is codegen parity, not a behavior change: `gcc -O2` applies the identical `printf(\"%s\\n\",s)` → `puts(s)` rewrite to `printLine`.\n\n`c_src/` sources are unmodified (checksums verified); the only addition there is the `build/` directory the documented cmake command creates."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1474,"cache_read_input_tokens":157139,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1474,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f9439a60-726c-49d3-8b44-096a552fc68e","uuid":"6bf77301-ccbc-47c4-8788-cae4c7da16da","timestamp":"2026-08-18T04:00:49.068Z"}
